+### 2a-bis. `lloyd/MEMORY.md` is an index (review 2026-09-24, P4)
+`lloyd/MEMORY.md` holds one typed line per entry; the detail lives in topic files `~/obsidian/lloyd/memory/<slug>.md`, which are never loaded into a prompt. Every write this job makes to it follows these rules:
+- **Typed entries only.** Add with `memory_add(file="MEMORY.md", type=…, entry=…)`, which writes `- [type] (YYYY-MM-DD) text`; `type` is `feedback` (a ruling or correction from Alan), `user`, `project` or `reference`. A hand-written line uses the same shape.
+- **Detail goes to the topic file, the index gets a pointer.** Proving commands, numbers and incident history go in `memory_add(file="topics/<slug>", entry=…)` (slug `[a-z0-9-]{1,48}`, one topic per subject — append to an existing topic before opening a new one), and the index line ends `→ topics/<slug>`.
+- **An index line is at most 300 characters**, pointer included. A `feedback` line is the ruling itself, complete — never a pointer to one.
+- **A refusal over the ceiling is stop-and-consolidate.** When `memory_add`/`Write`/`Edit` refuses MEMORY.md for size, do not trim ad hoc to squeeze the entry in: write the entry to its topic file, record `MEMORY.md at ceiling — consolidation owed` in the completion note, and leave the tightening to dream-consolidation (#47).
+- **No Bash heredocs, `cat >`, `sed -i` or `tee` for `lloyd/MEMORY.md` or `lloyd/USER.md`.** Bash is outside every tool that enforces the ceiling; the memory tools and `Edit` are the only writers.
